  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Moderate rain showers expected Sunday afternoon\ninto Monday morning but should have minimal impact on drought.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA northern stream trough tracks across southeastern Canada from\nSunday afternoon into late Sunday night, then crosses Maine\nfrom early Monday morning into early Monday evening. At the\nsurface a cold front will approach Sunday, then cross the area\nSunday night into Monday morning. This should bring some \nscattered to possibly numerous showers to the region from late \nSunday afternoon into Monday morning. The strongest forcing will\nbe across the North, with that area in the left rear quadrant \nof a developing 120+ kt upper jet. While surface based CAPE \nbelow 200 J/kg is expected across this area serves as a limiting\nfactor, mid-level lapse rates of 6.5-7 C/km are forecast. So \nsome elevated convection, possibly even a rumble of thunder in \nthe afternoon/early evening hours is possible. The best chance \nfor any (elevated) thunder is across the North Woods and just \noffshore.\n\nPrecipitable water values should range from 1.25 to 1.5 inches \nacross the North and 1.5 to 1.75 inches elsewhere. Around a\nthird of an inch of rain is likely along the coast and the St. \nJohn Valley, with lower amounts elsewhere. Given the very dry \nantecedent conditions, other than possibly some ponding of water\non roadways, no significant impacts are expected. As of now, \nthis is not expected to have any appreciable impact on ongoing \ndrought across the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Patchy/areas of frost possible across northern \nareas Monday night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure will build across the region behind the cold \nfront Monday, bringing clear skies and light winds. This will \nresult in favorable conditions for radiational cooling. Low \ntemperatures will range from the mid to upper 30s across much of\nnorthern Maine. Patchy/areas of frost are possible Monday night\nin the normally colder low lying locations.",
